<CounterfeitStudy
  title="Merit for Sale"
  subtitle="Go Deeper: The Rewards of the 38 Blessings"
  deck="Fine skin, long life, and the souls of men."
  description="What merit buys in The 38 Blessings, set beside Torah's covenant blessings and the three Christian twins: the prosperity gospel, indulgences, and purgatory."
>
    <h2>The Teaching</h2>
    <p>The closing chapters of <em>The 38 Blessings</em> show what merit buys. Keeping the First Precept, against taking life, "for the whole day is a way of merit making for the practitioner." The book lists the rewards: "living happily; awaking happily; devoid of bad dreams," being loved by mankind and non-humankind, being "protected by gods," "harmless by fire or poisons," "fine skin," a clear conscience, and being conscious at death. "In addition, the practitioner will hold a much longer lifespan with healthy physical condition."</p>
    <p>Merit can also be made on behalf of others. Thais have "great opportunity to make merit for themselves, the nation, the religion, and the King," and responding to "His Majesty's wish" is "an opportunity to gain merit for us and our families." Breaking a precept has its price as well. Those who break the precept against intoxicants "will be born into the underworld as demons, and serve their terms in hell," and afterward be reborn "as mentally impaired being." The book ties the Five Precepts to "Economic Sufficiency."</p>
    <h2>What the Word Says</h2>
    <p>Scripture does tie blessing to obedience, and an honest study has to say so. "If thou shalt hearken diligently unto the voice of Yahuah thy God... all these blessings shall come on thee" (Deuteronomy 28:1-2). The Fifth Commandment carries the promise "that thy days may be long" (Exodus 20:12). Reward for obedience is in Torah.</p>
    <p>The difference is in what the reward is and who gives it. Torah's blessings are covenant promises from a Person to His people, not wages paid out by a law of cause and effect. And no amount of obedience buys deliverance or another man's soul. "None of them can by any means redeem his brother, nor give to God a ransom for him" (Psalm 49:7). Yahuah's offer is "without money and without price" (Isaiah 55:1). When Simon the sorcerer tried to buy the gift of the Spirit, Peter answered, "Thy money perish with thee, because thou hast thought that the gift of God may be purchased with money" (Acts 8:20).</p>
    <h2>Three Twins in Christian Circles</h2>
    <p>The first is the prosperity gospel. Its preachers call giving to their ministry a "seed" that will return a hundredfold in money, health, and success. Paul named that teaching long ago: men "supposing that gain is godliness: from such withdraw thyself" (1 Timothy 6:5). "They that will be rich fall into temptation and a snare," for "the love of money is the root of all evil" (1 Timothy 6:9-10).</p>
    <p>The second is the treasury of merit and the indulgence. In 1343 Pope Clement VI, in the bull <em>Unigenitus</em>, described a treasury of merit gathered from Messiah and the saints, which the church could dispense. Indulgences drawn from that treasury were said to shorten punishment for sin, even for the dead. In 1517 Johann Tetzel sold them to raise money for St. Peter's Basilica in Rome, and that sale moved Martin Luther to post his Ninety-five Theses. Pope Pius V banned the sale of indulgences in 1567, but the Roman Church still grants them today. Merit made for the nation, the King, and the family in Thailand is the same idea under another flag.</p>
    <p>The third is purgatory. Rome teaches that the souls of the faithful dead are purified by suffering before entering heaven, and that the living can shorten that suffering with Masses, prayers, and indulgences. The teaching was formally defined at the Councils of Lyon (1274) and Florence (1439) and reaffirmed at Trent (1563). The book's "serve their terms in hell" and then return is the same shape: a set term of punishment that runs out, and then a way out.</p>
    <h2>Where It Turned</h2>
    <p>In every case, favor became a market. Paul draws the line between the two: "the wages of sin is death; but the gift of God is eternal life through Yahushua Messiah" (Romans 6:23). Wages are earned; a gift is not. Once merit can be banked, it can be counted, and once it can be counted, it can be sold.</p>
    <h2>The Verdict</h2>
    <p>Yahuah blesses obedience, and He promised to. But He never opened a bank where merit is deposited, transferred to the King, spent on fine skin, or used to pay down another man's sentence.</p>
    <p class="cfs-ready"><strong>Ready Answer.</strong> Torah blesses obedience as a covenant promise; merit-making, the prosperity gospel, and indulgences all turn that blessing into a purchase, and "the gift of God may" not "be purchased with money" (Acts 8:20).</p>
    <p>Revelation names the market and its last item. The merchants of Babylon weep over goods no one buys anymore, a list that ends with "slaves, and souls of men" (Revelation 18:13). The call that goes with it is plain: "Come out of her, my people" (Revelation 18:4).</p>
